grread-keyboard-event-is-a-list-not-a-dotted-pair — real BricsCAD grread returns (code value) as a proper list; a common downstream assumption (and this issue's own filer's code) wrongly treats it as (code . value)
======================================================================================================================================================================================================================

Status: DONE (2026-09-12, clautolisp 2.2.15 / alfe 2.2.23). grread is now
implemented for the KEYBOARD case (code 2): builtin-grread routes through
host-grread on the cador host, which reads one character from the prompt-stream
and returns the documented PROPER LIST (2 CHAR-CODE) — a two-element list, never
a dotted pair. A host with no interactive input (batch / unsupported backend)
returns nil. Mouse / point / tablet codes are out of scope (clautolisp has no
drawing editor; pjb: "keyboard code 2 across both UIs; skip mouse"). The seam is
UI-agnostic (the host prompt-stream), so it works under both the dumb and
ncurses front-ends and under --mock-input / test string streams. The autolisp-
spec GRREAD page gained a *** clautolisp deviation section and an explicit
list-not-dotted-pair note. Headless test grread-keyboard-event-is-a-proper-list
pins the shape (incl. the (cdr r) dotted-pair trap) and the batch→nil path; full
`make -C clautolisp test-sbcl` green. RESIDUAL (informational, needs real CAD,
NOT blocking — recorded here): the "To check" items below — whether AutoCAD
returns the same list shape (#1) and the mouse code-5 shape (#2) — remain
hardware-verification items; the implemented keyboard shape matches the
documented AutoLISP spec and live BricsCAD V26. Closed.

Original report — Found on real BricsCAD V26 (macOS) via a live bug report
in an unrelated project (autocad-gui-prototype), 2026-09-08. Not about
a clautolisp behavior difference this time (grread is a documented,
deliberate stub there -- returns NIL immediately, host cador doesn't
implement it, see clautolisp_grread_unimplemented in that project's
own notes) -- filed here as data for WHEN grread event synthesis is
eventually implemented, so the real return shape is right from the
start rather than needing a second fix once someone actually exercises
it.

Summary
-------

A custom keyboard-input loop (`(grread T 2 0)` in a `while` loop,
building up a string character by character -- the classic
DCL-free/inline-text-entry pattern) extracted the event's value via
`(cdr r)`, treating the grread return as a dotted pair
`(code . value)`. On real BricsCAD, a keyboard event actually comes
back as a genuine two-element LIST, `(code value)` -- so
`(cdr '(2 102))` is `(102)` (a one-element list), not `102` (the bare
integer). Downstream code passing that to `chr` fails loudly:

  bad argument type <(102)> ; expected <INTEGER> at [chr]

but code comparing it with `=` (e.g. `(= val 13)` for RETURN) does
NOT itself error -- `=` apparently just returns NIL comparing a list
against a number rather than raising a type error -- so a naive
version of this bug can silently mis-route control flow (every
special-key check quietly failing to match) rather than crashing
immediately, depending on what the code does in each branch. In the
reporting project's own case it happened to crash on the very last,
catch-all branch, which is what surfaced it -- a differently-shaped
handler could easily have masked this as "keystrokes are just being
ignored" instead, much harder to diagnose.

Why this is worth tracking now, unimplemented or not
------------------------------------------------------

- clautolisp's own grread already returns NIL for everything (a
  documented, intentional gap, not this issue) -- so this exact
  mismatch could never be exercised or caught headless under
  clautolisp today, by construction. The reporting project's own
  gui-inline-textbox-prompt has been in the codebase, "working" per
  every headless test, since roughly 2026-09-02/03 -- its very first
  live keystroke on real BricsCAD, over five days and 130+ headless
  tests later, is what finally hit this.
- If/when cador (or any future clautolisp host) implements real
  keyboard/mouse grread event synthesis, getting the return SHAPE
  right the first time (a plain list, not a dotted pair) avoids
  reproducing this exact class of bug for every project that then
  starts relying on clautolisp's own grread mock to validate
  real-world behavior it was previously untestable for.
- Worth checking the autolisp-spec's own GRREAD page for what it
  currently documents/implies about the return shape -- if it uses
  dotted-pair notation anywhere (a natural, common shorthand that
  reads ambiguously either way), that's worth correcting or at least
  disambiguating explicitly, given how easy the wrong reading is to
  fall into (the reporting project's own code, and this filer, both
  did).

To check
--------

  1. Real AutoCAD (not just BricsCAD) -- does its grread ALSO return a
     plain list `(code value)`, or does it differ? Not tested here
     (no AutoCAD access at hand for this specific check, unlike the
     read-string-does-not-skip-comments issue where the filer had
     both). Given grread is core, ancient AutoLISP (predates the
     AutoCAD/BricsCAD split entirely), a vendor divergence here would
     be surprising -- but "surprising" described the block-comment
     finding on that other issue too, so worth actually checking
     rather than assuming.
  2. Whether this list-vs-dotted-pair shape is consistent across EVERY
     grread event code (2=keyboard, 5=mouse-move, and whichever others
     exist), or varies by code. Only the keyboard case (code 2) has
     been directly observed on real hardware as of this filing; a
     second, analogous fix for code 5 (mouse-move, `val` expected to
     be a point) was applied downstream by the same reasoning but is
     NOT yet independently confirmed live.

Downstream fix (autocad-gui-prototype, not clautolisp itself)
---------------------------------------------------------------

`(setq code (car r) val (cadr r))` instead of `(setq code (car r) val
(cdr r))`, in both of that project's own grread consumers
(gui-inline-textbox-prompt, confirmed against the real error; and
gui-drag-attach-loop's identical pattern, fixed by the same reasoning
but not yet independently confirmed for its own code=5 case). See
gui-utils/src/gui-utils.lsp.

Found by
--------

autocad-gui-prototype gui-inline-textbox-prompt live-BricsCAD bug
report and fix, 2026-09-08 (Claude Sonnet 5, with pjb driving BricsCAD
interactively — same session, same day as the
read-string-does-not-skip-comments issue).
